  "question": {
    "text": "A small block of mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mtext>M</mtext></math> moves on a frictionless surface of an inclined plane, as shown in the figure. The angle of the incline suddenly changes from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>°</mo></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo>°</mo></math> at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>. The block is initially at rest at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>. Assume that collisions between the block and the incline are totally inelastic (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mo>&nbsp;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></math>). Figure:<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/kjfweL12.png\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 157px;\" />​<br />\r<br />\rThe speed of the block at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math>, immediately before it leaves the second incline is -",
    "image": null
  },
